  "concurrency": 8,
  "design": {
    "question": "The shipped ranker adds a citation prior at weight 0.08 and a recency prior at weight 0.04 on top of the relevance signal. This measures whether either one earns its weight on known-item retrieval.",
    "method": "Each arm is a RE-SCORING, in JavaScript, of the per-signal breakdown the service already returns on every row (signals = {semantic, lexical, citation, recency}). No production code was modified and nothing was deployed. One live retrieval per query per mode supplies the candidate pool, and every arm re-sorts THAT SAME pool, so the pool is held identical across arms and a weight change cannot be confounded with a change in what was retrieved.",
    "exactness": "The re-scoring is not an approximation of the ranker, it is the ranker: recomputing 1.00*semantic + 0.10*lexical + 0.08*citation + 0.04*recency reproduces the service's own ordering key (rawScore) to floating-point noise. This is asserted on every row of every query before any result is written, together with a check that the full arm reproduces the service's own top-10 order. The one departure from double-precision noise is the lexical term, which is a float4 on the wire — see correctness_gate.float4_note.",
    "pool": "topK=400 was requested because the SQL scores a candidate pool and then applies ORDER BY final_score DESC LIMIT topK. retrieveCount = min(400, max(200, topK*4)), so at topK=400 the pool and the limit are both 400 on the vector path and nothing is truncated. A smaller topK would have returned only the shipped-weight winners, which no re-ranking could undo.",
    "tie_rule": "Exact score ties are broken by the row's original position in what the service returned, so two arms can never disagree by tie order alone.",
    "pool_widths": {
      "pool_400": "Primary. Everything the service returned, in the order it returned it.",
      "pool_200": "The first 200 rows by the mode's own first-stage retrieval key — embedding similarity for the vector path, text rank for keyword mode — which is the candidate pool production actually holds at topK=10, since the service retrieves at least 200 candidates and the pool is chosen by retrieval distance rather than by final score."
    },
    "keyword_formula": "Keyword mode computes no vector, so signals.semantic is null and the shipped score is 1.00*lexical + 0.08*citation + 0.04*recency. Its lexical term is the relevance signal rather than a prior, so no_lexical and similarity_only are not defined for it.",
    "holm": "Holm-Bonferroni is reported twice. mcnemar_p_holm corrects within one mode at one pool width; mcnemar_p_holm_mode_wide corrects across both pool widths within a mode. Each comparison names its own family in holm_family.",
    "mcnemar_rule": "Exact binomial, doubled one-sided tail (p = min(1, 2*tail)), from benchmarks/scripts/stats.mjs."
  },
  "disclosures": [
    "The candidate pool is 400 candidates wide, which is WIDER than the 200 production retrieves at topK=10. That is the price of an untruncated pool. The pool_200 figures re-run every arm over production's actual width and are reported alongside.",
    "The corpus was read with a 30 s statement timeout.",
    "The lexical prior is max-normalised by the SQL over the pool it scored. For the vector path that was 400 rows, not 200, so the pool_200 figures are exact only where the lexical prior is zero on every row — which is measured, not assumed: see findings.lexical_prior_occupancy. Where the lexical prior is alive, the pool_200 lexical values may be scaled slightly differently from what production would compute, and the pool_200 row is a close approximation rather than an identity.",
    "Keyword mode scores 1,600 candidates and returns topK, so its visible pool is itself a shipped-weight truncation and its pool_200 reconstruction is approximate. The lexical normalisation is unaffected (the maximum text rank is in every prefix of a text-rank-ordered pool), and the truncation is quantified in findings.keyword_truncation.",
    "A decline is scored as a complete miss, identically for every arm. The hybrid mode declines when the quoted term is too common to narrow anything; keyword mode declines when a query would have to rank a large fraction of the corpus. Declines are counted separately and are NOT dropped from the denominator, because a user who is declined receives nothing.",
    "Known-item retrieval is the only thing measured here. An arm that loses no known-item ranks may still change the character of a result list, which is why the citation profile and the undated-row counts are reported next to the metrics."
  ],
